      "content": "DNS propagation is controlled by the TTL settings, which dictate how long DNS records are cached by servers and ISPs globally. If someone has a lower TTL, they might see the changes faster, but this also means that you need to be ready for those changes to go away sooner. Geographic distribution of DNS servers, load on specific DNS resolvers, and how you've configured your DNS settings can all lead to variances in propagation times. Consistency usually improves when a multi-tier approach is taken, using different nameservers and checking the status with different networks. Sometimes reaching out to your ISP or a third-party DNS propagation checking tool can help identify any ongoing issues.",
